!! Content-Security-Policy (optional)

`twmigrate.py migrate --csp` adds a CSP `<meta>`: inline scripts only by hash, `connect-src` limited to this site and the GitHub API origin, no external images/frames, no `<object>`. `'unsafe-eval'` has to stay because TiddlyWiki loads its own modules with `eval`. Consequences: external images/`_canonical_uri` files, embedded third-party iframes and the online plugin library stop working unless you extend the policy in the generated `tw.html`. Changing `tw.html` (any plugin update) means re-running `migrate`/`rotate`, which recomputes the hashes.

!! Publishing a note as a blog post

# Write the note as usual (Markdown is the default type; WikiText works too if it converts cleanly).
# Press the ''globe'' button in the note's toolbar. If the note is not a blog post yet, you are asked once whether to turn it into one (this only adds the tag `publish:blog`).
# A preview shows the exact Markdown that becomes ''public'' and where (`content/blog/<slug>/index.md`, or `index.de.md` for German). Press //Publish…// and confirm the browser dialog. The website rebuilds in about a minute.
# A status bar under the note now shows the live link, //Update…// (publish the current version again) and //Unpublish…//.

Filled in automatically: title, date (created), last update (modified), a description from your first paragraph, the URL (from the title, fixed after the first publish), reading time (by the website).

Optional, in the //Blog post// panel of the editor (shown for tagged notes): //Summary// (`summary`), //Post tags// (`publish-tags`; your vault tags are never copied), //Language// (`publish-lang`: en/de), //URL// (`publish-slug`), //Images// (`publish-media`, only for images not used in the text).

Images: import a PNG/JPEG/WebP as an image tiddler and use it in the text as `![alt text](#Image title)` or `[img[Image title]]`; it is published next to the post automatically. External image URLs are refused (the website only shows its own images).

Links: `[[Other note]]` becomes a link if that note is published, otherwise plain text. Refused (they could pull in private notes or run code): transclusions `{{…}}`, macros `<<…>>`, widgets `<$…>`, raw HTML, pragmas; inside code blocks they are fine. Invisible or bidi-control Unicode is refused too.

German and English versions of one post: give both notes the same //URL// (`publish-slug`) and set //Language// to Deutsch on the German one.

See the threat model, section //Publishing//.
